<Project>

  <!--
    The gate of ADR-0038. The analyzer assemblies sit in dcat-analyzers/ rather than in
    analyzers/dotnet/cs/, so NuGet resolves nothing on its own and this file is the ONLY thing that
    ever hands them to a compiler. What that costs is exactly what it buys: the flow stops being
    NuGet's and becomes ours, so it can end where we say it ends rather than at the edge of the
    dependency graph.

    buildTransitive/ and NOT build/. This file has to reach a project that references a CATALOGUE,
    where the foundation is a transitive dependency and a build/ folder is never imported. It is
    also the only copy: measured, a package carrying buildTransitive/ has its build/ folder ignored
    entirely — for direct consumers too — so a second copy under build/ would simply never be read.

    ONE condition rather than a property plus an item group, because MSBuild evaluates every
    property before any item, and a property condition may therefore not read @(PackageReference).
    At evaluation time rather than inside a target, so a design-time build sees the analyzers and
    the IDE offers the code fixes.

      first clause    something asked for the analyzers, and that something is normally a
                      catalogue: every catalogue packs build/<its own id>.props
                      (Directory.Build.targets), which NuGet imports for a DIRECT reference and for
                      nothing further out. That is what stops an application inheriting
                      error-severity diagnostics from a library that merely used a catalogue for
                      its own suppressions.

      second clause   the foundation referenced directly, with no catalogue in sight — a catalogue
                      author declaring rules of their own, who wants the definition diagnostics.
                      @(PackageReference) holds the building project's own references, so this is
                      the one place that case can be recognised.

    Both clauses READ EnableDiagnosticCatalogAnalyzers and neither assigns it, so a project that
    set it means it: true to be checked by a catalogue it only reaches indirectly, false to keep a
    catalogue and decline the analysis. That second lever is the one a single package took away
    (ADR-0037's fifth negative consequence) and this arrangement gives back, without asking anyone
    to silence a diagnostic in .editorconfig.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(EnableDiagnosticCatalogAnalyzers)' == 'true' or ('$(EnableDiagnosticCatalogAnalyzers)' == '' and '@(PackageReference->WithMetadataValue('Identity','DiagnosticCatalog'))' != '')">
    <Analyzer Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../dcat-analyzers/DiagnosticCatalog.Analyzers.dll" />
    <Analyzer Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../dcat-analyzers/DiagnosticCatalog.CodeFixes.dll" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    DCAT0015 reports a catalogue that publishes without the opt-in above, and that is a fact about a
    .csproj: no syntax tree, symbol or metadata reference carries it, so the analyzer cannot see it and
    something has to tell it. These two properties are that telling. CompilerVisibleProperty is the
    supported channel — the SDK writes each one into the generated AnalyzerConfig as build_property.*.

    PackageId travels because the message names the FILE to add, and build/<package id>.props is the
    only name NuGet imports. The assembly name is not a safe stand-in: the two differ often, and a
    message naming the wrong file is worse than no message.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn" />
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="PackageId" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Runs before the SDK writes that AnalyzerConfig, which is the last moment a property can still reach
    an analyzer.

    GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFileCORE, not GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFile. The latter is a wrapper
    whose DependsOnTargets names the former, so a BeforeTargets hook on it fires AFTER the file has
    already been written — measured, and the symptom is the worst kind: the property is computed
    correctly, the analyzer receives an empty value, and the diagnostic simply never fires.

    Deliberately narrow. It classifies nothing unless the project actually publishes a package with an
    id, so an application, a test project or an unpackable library is never a candidate and never has
    to be excluded later. And it computes nothing when the property already has a value: a catalogue
    that arranges the opt-in in some way the match below cannot see writes
    <DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn>packed</DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn> and is believed. That
    escape exists because what follows is a match against how a file was DECLARED, and MSBuild offers
    more than one spelling of the same packaging.

    Three spellings are recognised, and they are the three people write:
      PackagePath="build/Acme.Catalog.props"    the explicit path
      PackagePath="build\Acme.Catalog.props"    the same, on Windows
      PackagePath="build"                       the folder, with the file already named for the package

    Anything else reads as missing, which is the conservative direction: a false "missing" is a warning
    on a correct package, visible and one property away from silence, while a false "packed" would be
    this diagnostic failing at the one job it has.
  -->
  <Target Name="DiagnosticCatalogClassifyAnalyzerOptIn"
          BeforeTargets="GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFileCore"
          Condition="'$(IsPackable)' == 'true' and '$(PackageId)' != '' and '$(DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn)' == ''">

    <ItemGroup>
      <DiagnosticCatalogOptInCandidate
          Include="@(None)"
          Condition="'%(None.Pack)' == 'true' and (
                       '%(None.PackagePath)' == 'build/$(PackageId).props' or
                       '%(None.PackagePath)' == 'build\$(PackageId).props' or
                       (('%(None.PackagePath)' == 'build' or
                         '%(None.PackagePath)' == 'build/' or
                         '%(None.PackagePath)' == 'build\') and
                        '%(None.Filename)%(None.Extension)' == '$(PackageId).props'))" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <PropertyGroup>
      <DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn Condition="'@(DiagnosticCatalogOptInCandidate)' != ''">packed</DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn>
      <DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn Condition="'$(DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn)' == ''">missing</DiagnosticCatalogAnalyzerOptIn>
    </PropertyGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
